3070 #edb/ref 1061 38037 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163070 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ae3ab16a-d830-544c-8331-c62d1505d7dd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163070 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186162107 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163071 #edb/ref 1061 38040 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163071 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6c760552-8a9d-54b8-962b-87e6f849c3da" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163071 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186162108 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163086 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186163070 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163086 #edb/ref 1061 38149 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163086 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2f745e7f-72c3-508a-96ad-ef71ea659d96" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163086 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186163333 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163087 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186163071 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163087 #edb/ref 1061 38150 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163087 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c79d4341-23ea-55d9-84aa-e073291790d1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163087 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186163334 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163156 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186163159 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163156 #edb/ref 1061 44847 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163156 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b566ab50-5b2d-5693-aaa2-b74aeb985d53" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163156 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186163365 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163157 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186163158 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163157 #edb/ref 1061 44848 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163157 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "85e8d07f-ff5e-5a08-b15e-b9682157622a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163157 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186162153 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163158 #edb/ref 1061 44851 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163158 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3bf9c7bd-4281-5c38-aa27-271d95010465" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163158 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186162154 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163159 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186163157 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163159 #edb/ref 1061 44854 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163159 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b0b7073a-7020-52ef-b9e8-f169d03cd0f4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163159 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186162155 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163179 #edb/ref 1064 "We can do the same trick using the **elementary column operations** instead of row operations.\n\nThere are three types of elementary *column* operations, which are entirely analogous to the corresponding row operations:\n\n- Switching any two columns: \n\n$$\nC_i \\leftrightarrow C_j\n$$\n\n- Multiplying (each element of) a column by a non-zero number: \n\n$$\nC_i := k C_i,\\qquad k \\neq 0\n$$\n\n- Adding a multiple of one column to another column: \n\n$$\nC_j := C_j+k C_i,\\qquad j \\neq i\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** \n\n- Unlike elementary row operations, elementary *column* operations change the solutions of a system of linear equations. This is because column operations alter the coefficients associated with each variable across the system, changing the equations.\n\n- The exception is swapping two columns - this doesn't alter the solutions *provided that* we swap the corresponding variables in the solution vector. For example, if we swap the first and second columns, we must treat the second variable as the first and vice versa. The solution set remains unchanged because the relationships between the variables and their coefficients are preserved." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163179 #edb/ref 1065 10652 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163179 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "e947134c-e909-5a3b-85a6-d284e04816e0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163179 #edb/ref 1067 "Column Operations" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163214 #edb/ref 1064 "When studying linear algebra, it is common to use elementary row operations to reduce a matrix $A$ to another matrix $B$ such that $B$ contains many zeros. \n\nTo illustrate, let's consider the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -5 & -3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the entry $a_{11}$ located in the top left-hand corner. We can reduce *all* of the entries *below* it using elementary row operations.\n\nFirst, we subtract $3$ times the first row from the second row.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A= & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -5 & -3 & 6\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}: &={R}_{2}-3{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\ ∼ & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 10 \\\\ -5 & -3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we add $5$ times the first row to the third row.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 10 \\\\ -5 & -3 & 6\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}: &={R}_{3}+5{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\ ∼ & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -14\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we're done! We have successfully performed elementary row operations to reduce all of the entries below $a_{11}$ to zero." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186163214 #edb/ref 1065 4828 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163214 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "1e0cae18-aa8d-5b1d-9aab-4a5334dec0c0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163214 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163326 #edb/ref 1064 "Recall that if $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with mean $\\mu$ and variance $\\sigma^2,$ then the sample mean, denoted $\\overline{X},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overline{X} = \\dfrac1n\\sum_{i=1}^n X_i.\n$$\n\nThe sample mean is a random variable whose probability distribution (also called the **sampling distribution**) has an expected value of $\\mu\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[\\overline{X}] = \\mu\n$$\n\n \n\nIn other words, the sample mean $\\overline{X}$ is an unbiased estimate of the population mean $\\mu.$\n\nLet's now find the variance of the sampling distribution of $\\overline{X}.$ Using the properties of variance for sums of independent random variables, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}] & =\\text{Var}[\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}] \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{n})}^{2}\\cdot \\text{Var}[{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}\\cdot \\text{Var}[{X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{n}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}\\cdot (\\text{Var}[{X}_{1}]+\\text{Var}[{X}_{2}]+⋯+\\text{Var}[{X}_{n}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}\\cdot ({\\sigma }^{2}+{\\sigma }^{2}+⋯+{\\sigma }^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}\\cdot n{\\sigma }^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo summarize, we have the following important result:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[\\overline{X}] = \\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{n}\n$$\n\nIt should be noted that we don't yet know the sampling distribution of $\\overline{X}.$ What we *do* know is that whatever the sampling distribution of $\\overline{X}$ is, it has mean $\\mu$ and variance $\\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{n}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163326 #edb/ref 1065 6276 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163326 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "58763c8a-23b3-55bb-9b5c-4781a3c271b4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163326 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163333 #edb/ref 1064 "The standard deviation of the sample mean is called the **standard error** of the sample mean and is denoted $\\textrm{SE}[\\overline{X}]\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\textrm{SE}[\\overline{X}] = \\sqrt{\\textrm{Var}[\\overline{X}]}\n$$\n\nSo, if we have a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with mean $\\mu$ and variance $\\sigma^2,$ then the  standard error is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{SE}[\\overline{X}] = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{n}} = \\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt n}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163333 #edb/ref 1065 6300 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163333 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "c2217692-f38f-597c-9295-cd25f1d4d026" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163333 #edb/ref 1067 "The Standard Error" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163334 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we have a random sample ![](images/ff/ff40ddf134336ef15bcaf81b5b23aa8fb7c5ea13276e0f86fa6dde15841e0c5d.svg) of size ![](images/fb/fb2a8560bbc7122a4dd4f1a0bb9862d4368a63f4b03968884a3a60e7423a852c.svg) from a population with mean ![](images/75/75d2faaecd7646e08d87276399a471f8f713fbeda95e358e94410ac2e23b42a5.svg) and variance ![](images/1a/1af459c3f0307649888db8b148eef968a9c5da6a77788c4ab7fe32d6ea30717e.svg) We know that the sample mean \n\n![](images/51/513b88bd8fa93336cc949758da79cac238f871b477d1682ab6b20635a4149361.svg)\n\nis an unbiased estimator of ![](images/98/9878f65a2daf16e1fbd2b0c4929e4407c4ddc406c147dab8a5f6b1a8ee25af33.svg) \n\nHowever, it should be noted that selecting a single observation ![](images/3c/3c8b064868e0622effd6b83427f453ecdb3e033319c9e02969bb988b22ec7cb6.svg) to represent the whole could *also* be considered an unbiased estimator of the population mean. Recall that, by assumption\n\n![](images/e7/e7cc0631f5e6b060f0b9fc77dcac428c2b853d0e19be2e9b20e6e9f8d5d7aaad.svg)\n\nIn what sense is ![](images/84/84269b1ed65c2a169ac4246065d27cbba714010309ddb03a65f7fa878b7b65ea.svg) a \"better\" estimator of ![](images/75/75d2faaecd7646e08d87276399a471f8f713fbeda95e358e94410ac2e23b42a5.svg) than, say, ![](images/c3/c3bb2b6f3769acd8b9c9ce9f929b5bc6c7ec840ab18503cf4c878a1fe8f8f41d.svg) The answer lies in their respective variances. \n\nAs the sample size ![](images/fb/fb2a8560bbc7122a4dd4f1a0bb9862d4368a63f4b03968884a3a60e7423a852c.svg) becomes larger and larger, the variance and standard error of ![](images/84/84269b1ed65c2a169ac4246065d27cbba714010309ddb03a65f7fa878b7b65ea.svg) become smaller and smaller. It is easy to see that, as ![](images/f4/f4d85c2aac179db9322aef2478cfa080e9d602f8aeb8c0436f646f0e2321c139.svg)\n we have\n![](images/d5/d5676c70b4fcb7f68704ba0bb46fc262c7d8f764748e252aa989d6e82e88283b.svg)\n\nSo, when the sample size ![](images/fb/fb2a8560bbc7122a4dd4f1a0bb9862d4368a63f4b03968884a3a60e7423a852c.svg) is large, there is less variance in the sampling distribution of ![](images/dc/dc01b2699b68bb8be585039efc249eaeeba5e4ad17a5c687a8811bf266cabc87.svg) Therefore, the larger the sample, the more likely it is that ![](images/84/84269b1ed65c2a169ac4246065d27cbba714010309ddb03a65f7fa878b7b65ea.svg) will give good estimates for ![](images/98/9878f65a2daf16e1fbd2b0c4929e4407c4ddc406c147dab8a5f6b1a8ee25af33.svg) The individual sample elements do not have this property since ![](images/63/631e2156cfdd9541694e1238fb8c717e721e0ef452de4bb898beb2c4509abc6b.svg)\n\nThe key takeaway is that the sample mean ![](images/84/84269b1ed65c2a169ac4246065d27cbba714010309ddb03a65f7fa878b7b65ea.svg) becomes a better estimator for the corresponding population mean ![](images/75/75d2faaecd7646e08d87276399a471f8f713fbeda95e358e94410ac2e23b42a5.svg) as the sample size ![](images/fb/fb2a8560bbc7122a4dd4f1a0bb9862d4368a63f4b03968884a3a60e7423a852c.svg) increases." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163334 #edb/ref 1065 6301 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163334 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "2fc54f89-7780-558c-8193-28d92a025bc8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163334 #edb/ref 1067 "The Sample Mean and Large Samples" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163365 #edb/ref 1064 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be discrete random variables with supports $S_X$ and $S_Y,$ respectively. We say that $X$ and $Y$ are **independent** if, for all $x\\in S_X$ and $y\\in S_Y,$ we have \n\n$$\nP(X=x, Y=y)=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y).\n$$\n\nMoreover, if $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y),$ and $f_X(x)$ and $f_Y(y)$ are the marginal mass functions of $X$ and $Y,$ respectively, then\n\n$$\nf(x, y) = f_X(x)\\cdot f_Y (y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all $x\\in S_X$ and $y\\in S_Y.$\n\nIf the random variables are not independent, they are **dependent**." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163365 #edb/ref 1065 7746 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163365 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "ab3a2705-ec4d-5e72-b6dd-553e9cbb6884" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [175921861